      "text": "**Madame Bovary** by Gustave Flaubert was first published in 1856 in the literary magazine *Revue de Paris*. The novel follows Emma Bovary, a doctor's wife in provincial Normandy, who seeks fulfillment through romantic affairs and extravagant spending, ultimately leading to her downfall. Flaubert's use of free indirect discourse allows readers to experience Emma's romantic aspirations while also maintaining an ironic distance from her life choices.\n\nThe publication of *Madame Bovary* was not without controversy. Upon its release, the novel faced criticism for its depictions of infidelity and its critique of bourgeois values. Flaubert, along with his publisher, was tried for obscenity shortly after its publication. The trial concluded with a judgment of not guilty, but the affair significantly heightened the novel's profile and cemented its place in literary history.\n\nIn summary:\n- **Published**: 1856 in *Revue de Paris*\n- **Key Themes**: Romanticism vs. realism, desire and disillusionment, gender constraints\n- **Controversy**: Flaubert and his publisher faced trial for obscenity due to the novel's content.\n\nFor further details, you can refer to the [Wikipedia page on Madame Bovary](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Madame_Bovary).",
